"""You can see the description of the task using the code specified in the title on leetcode.
This program has O(n * log(n)) complexity by time and O(n) by space.

As with the previous problem, the key insight lies in understanding why this simple code yields the correct answer.
Regardless of the target value we choose to transform our array elements toward, 
the total number of steps required - summing the distances from the leftmost and rightmost elements (in a sorted array) - remains constant. 
If we decrease the target value, the number of steps required from the right side increases, and vice versa. 
Consequently, the problem reduces to finding the median of all the elements.

There is a solution that goes beyond the standard approach (O(n)) - by finding a certain statistic - but I’m wary of it.
"""

class Solution:
    def minMoves2(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:
        nums.sort()
        med = nums[len(nums) // 2] 
        return sum(abs(elem - med) for elem in nums)
